Uses only Node builtins. Good.

Now let me verify that all the Done-when lines pass. Let me also double-check that the test asserts exact state equality, not weaker patterns. Let me look more carefully at the test:

```ts
async function paint(page: Page, state: GameState) {
  expect(await page.locator("[data-country]").evaluateAll(nodes => Object.fromEntries(nodes.map(n => [n.getAttribute("data-country"), [n.getAttribute("data-owner"), Number(n.getAttribute("data-armies"))]])))).toEqual(Object.fromEntries(state.map.provinces.map(p => [p.id, [state.ownerOf[p.id], state.armies[p.id]]])));
}
```

This uses `.toEqual()` for deep equality. That's strict.

```ts
expect(deserializeVersionedMatch(await save(page)).options).toEqual(fixture.options);
expect(deserializeVersionedMatch(text).state).toEqual(state);
```

Same - exact equality on options and state.

```ts
expect(state.phase).toBe("finished"); expect(state.winner).toBe("seat1"); expect(state.result!.reason).toBe("conquest");
```

Strict equality.

```ts
expect(index).toBe(fixture.expected.actions);
expect([resumed, moved, battle, computer, eliminated]).toEqual([true,true,true,true,true]);
```

Strict equality on count and observations.

The test doesn't weaken anything. Let me also check the test handles the fact that some captures may not have amount (computer turns). Let me re-read:

```ts
do { state = step(state, fixture.actions[index++]!); steps++; } while (index < fixture.actions.length && activePlayer(state)!.kind === "computer" && state.phase !== "finished");
```

For computer turns, it just calls step() on each action. This works because apply() doesn't require amount for captureMove (it's optional in the type).

But wait, let me check more carefully: in the test, for human captureMove:
```ts
} else if (action.kind === "captureMove") {
  ...
  await page.locator("#capture-amount").fill(String(action.amount)); await page.locator("#confirm-capture-button").click();
}
state = step(state, action);
```

`action.amount` is accessed - so this would crash if `action.amount` is undefined. But that's only for human actions, and human captureMove actions all have amount (79 of them).

